From 0748720b620555f8c222782dcab575cf0cf403b4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: karfau <christian.bewernitz@bettermarks.com>
Date: Mon, 17 Aug 2026 23:41:20 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] CVE-2026-83614: bound malformed-tag recovery and O(K) normalize() text merge (upstream 0748720)
 adjacent-text merge (GHSA-93r5-fhx6-vmg9)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two quadratic-time recovery paths shared the single-character error-recovery trigger.

Finding A: `parseElementStartPart` scanned a malformed tag name forward to the next
`>` before rejecting it, and one-character recovery repeated that O(n) scan O(n)
times, so a document with many `<` inside a malformed tag parsed in O(n^2). A `<`
can never occur inside a tag name, so the scan now throws the distinct
`unexpected < in tag name: <candidate>` at the `<`, bounding each recovery step;
the new message is registered in `REPORTED`. DOM output is byte-identical — only the
reported error text (and mechanical warning-index positions) change.

Finding B: `endDocument` runs `normalize()`, whose adjacent-text merge did a
per-sibling `removeChild` (each an O(K) re-index) plus `appendData` (each an O(K)
string rebuild), so a run of K single-character text nodes cost O(K^2). The merge
now gathers a run, concatenates once, unlinks the merged siblings, and re-indexes a
single time — O(K). The first text node of each run survives with the concatenated
data (node identity / locator semantics preserved). This also fixes the O(K^2) on
the programmatic `createTextNode` + `normalize()` path, which no parser change covers.

https://github.com/xmldom/xmldom/security/advisories/GHSA-93r5-fhx6-vmg9
---
 lib/dom.js | 35 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++++---
 lib/sax.js |  9 +++++++++
 2 files changed, 41 insertions(+), 3 deletions(-)

diff --git a/lib/dom.js b/lib/dom.js
index c842f25..5731bed 100644
--- a/lib/dom.js
+++ b/lib/dom.js
@@ -1430,9 +1430,38 @@ Node.prototype = {
 				while (child) {
 					var next = child.nextSibling;
 					if (next !== null && next.nodeType === TEXT_NODE && child.nodeType === TEXT_NODE) {
-						node.removeChild(next);
-						child.appendData(next.data);
-						// Do not advance child: re-check new nextSibling for another text run
+						// Merge the whole run of adjacent text nodes at once: gather the
+						// following text siblings' data, unlink them in a single pass, and
+						// re-index the child list a single time. Per-sibling `removeChild`
+						// (each an O(K) re-index) plus per-sibling `appendData` (each an O(K)
+						// string rebuild) is O(K^2) over a long run of single-character text
+						// nodes; this keeps it O(K). The first text node of the run survives
+						// and carries the concatenated data, preserving node identity and
+						// locator semantics.
+						var tail = [];
+						var sibling = next;
+						while (sibling !== null && sibling.nodeType === TEXT_NODE) {
+							tail.push(sibling.data);
+							sibling = sibling.nextSibling;
+						}
+						// `sibling` is now the first non-text node after the run, or null.
+						var removed = child.nextSibling;
+						while (removed !== sibling) {
+							var following = removed.nextSibling;
+							removed.parentNode = null;
+							removed.previousSibling = null;
+							removed.nextSibling = null;
+							removed = following;
+						}
+						child.nextSibling = sibling;
